    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> pretended to be weak, gave up the Pratzen Heights and thinned his right flank, so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> weakened their own centre to attack it; Soult's corps then took the Heights.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Davout</a>'s III Corps marched 110 km in 48 hours from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to hold the French right against a much larger force.</li><li>Figures differ: one account gives the Allied army as 85,000 and the French as 72,000, another 89,000 and 66,000, and the Allied losses are given as about 36,000 against about 9,000 French.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s letter to Joséphine spoke of a victory over the army commanded by 'the two emperors', which gave the battle its name of the Battle of the Three Emperors, though Emperor Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was not present on the field.</p></aside>
    </section>

