    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">September 1805</span><span class="rf-text">200,000 French troops begin to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> on a front of 260 km</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">October 1805</span><span class="rf-text">23,000 Austrian troops surrender at Ulm</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">November 1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> falls, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> sets a trap by seeming weak and abandoning the Pratzen Heights</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">December 1805</span><span class="rf-text">The Allied council adopts Weyrother’s plan, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> hides his troops behind the Bosenitz stream</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">The French take the Pratzen Heights and break the Allied centre, and the Allied army abandons the field</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> sign a truce on 4 December</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1805</span><span class="rf-text">The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> takes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> out of the war on 26 December</span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-austerlitz" data-v="1966ef1b8d" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The armies take their ground</b> Napoleon abandoned the Pratzen Heights and moved all his troops but the right flank into dead ground. The Allies planned four columns against the French right, with the Russian Guard in reserve and Bagration on their right.</li><li><b>The Allied columns attack the French right</b> The first Allied lines attacked Telnitz and drove the French across the Goldbach. The first men of Davout's corps arrived and threw the Allies out of the village, and French artillery checked further attacks.</li><li><b>The Tsar orders Kutuzov off the Pratzen Heights</b> Kutuzov's corps had halted on the Heights, but the Tsar ordered it to withdraw. That left the Allied centre weak.</li><li><b>Soult's corps climbs the Heights</b> As the mist cleared, Napoleon ordered the attack. Soult's corps went up the slope and the Allies sent the delayed detachments of the fourth column into the fight. After over an hour of fighting the French bayoneted the Allies off the Heights.</li><li><b>The Russian Guard counterattacks and breaks</b> Grand Duke Constantine's Guard counterattacked in Vandamme's section and forced a bloody effort. French Guard cavalry and Bernadotte's I Corps came up, and the Russians broke and were pursued for about a quarter of a mile.</li><li><b>In the north the French drive off the cavalry and Bagration</b> Liechtenstein's heavy cavalry attacked Murat's lighter cavalry, and French cuirassiers finished off the Russian cavalry after a long and bitter mêlée. Lannes then led his corps against Bagration and drove him off the field.</li><li><b>Sokolnitz falls and the Allied army leaves the field</b> St. Hilaire's division and part of Davout's corps broke through at Sokolnitz. The commanders of the first two columns fled, and general panic seized the Allied army, which abandoned the field in all possible directions.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> had been in turmoil since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> began in 1792. In 1797 the French Republic broke up the First Coalition, and a Second Coalition formed in 1798 was defeated by 1801, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the only opponent of the French Consulate. Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> agreed to end hostilities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amiens">Treaty of Amiens</a> in March 1802, but problems persisted. The British resented returning captured colonies, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was angry that the British refused to abandon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>, and the situation worsened when he sent an expeditionary force to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. In May 1803 Britain declared war on France.</p><p>In December 1804 an Anglo-Swedish agreement led to the Third Coalition. By April 1805 Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> had signed an alliance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, defeated twice in recent memory and keen on revenge, joined a few months later. In August Napoleon, Emperor of the French since December 1804, turned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-channel">English Channel</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. On 20 October 23,000 Austrians surrendered at Ulm, bringing the Austrian prisoners of the campaign to 60,000, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> fell in November.</p><p>Napoleon's long lines of communication and the unknown intentions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> left him needing a battle. Kutuzov planned to retreat further, towards the Carpathian region. Napoleon set a trap: he gave the impression that his army was weak and wanted peace, and on 27 November ordered Soult to abandon Austerlitz and the Pratzen Heights in apparent disorder. Many Allied officers, including the Austrian chief of staff Franz von Weyrother, urged an immediate attack, and Kutuzov's plan was rejected.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Austerlitz&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Third Coalition:</p><ul><li>P.W. Schroeder, The Transformation of European Politics, 1763–1848 (Clarendon Press, 1996)</li><li>J.D. Grainger, The Amiens Truce: Britain and Bonaparte, 1801–1803 (Boydell Press, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout.jpg" alt="Louis-Nicolas Davout" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Louis-Nicolas Davout</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louis_nicolas_davout.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">After Pierre-Claude Gautherot / Tito Marzocchi de Bellucci, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a> had been the force assembled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> for a planned invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and it was organised in seven corps, each with 36 to 40 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and able to act alone until others came to its aid. By 1805 it had grown to 350,000 men. At Austerlitz <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had some 72,000 men and 157 guns, with about 7,000 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Davout</a> still far to the south. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> had about 85,000, seventy per cent of them Russian, and 318 guns. Another figure gives the armies as 89,000 and 66,000.</p><p>The Russian army had no permanent formation above the regiment, its senior officers were mostly aristocrats and its junior officers often undertrained, but its soldiers were steadfast and its artillery fine. The Austrian army had begun reforms under Archduke Charles, who lost influence when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> went to war against his advice; Karl Mack instituted further changes. Its cavalry was good but often outnumbered, and its artillery was the best in the Coalition but no better than the French.</p><p>The Allies adopted Weyrother's plan: a main drive on the French right, which looked lightly guarded, with diversions against the French left, in four columns. The Russian Imperial Guard was held in reserve and Bagration guarded the Allied right. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> took the command from Kutuzov and gave it to Weyrother. Napoleon weakened his right on purpose and hid his main force in dead ground. Davout was to hold the Goldbach first with 5,000 and then 12,500 men against Buxhoeveden's 43,000, while Soult's 16,000 struck the centre.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Austerlitz&#8221; cites where it covers French Imperial army, Russian Imperial army, Austrian Imperial army, Allied plans and dispositions:</p><ul><li>T. Fisher, The Napoleonic Wars: The Rise and Fall of an Empire (Osprey, 2004)</li><li>Gregory Fremont-Barnes, Napoleon Bonaparte: leadership, strategy, conflict (Osprey Publishing, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle began at about 8 a.m. with Allied attacks on Telnitz, held by the 3rd Line Regiment. Heavy fighting drove the French out, the first men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-nicolas-davout">Davout</a>'s corps arrived and threw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> out, and artillery checked further attacks. The Allied columns came on more slowly than intended. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liechtenstein">Liechtenstein</a>'s cavalry, moved from the left to the right, slowed part of the second column. Sokolnitz, defended by the 26th Light Regiment and skirmishers, changed hands several times.</p><p>Meanwhile Kutuzov's IV Corps stopped on the Pratzen Heights, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> ordered it to withdraw. At about 8:45 a.m. the mist was clearing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> saw the weakness of the Allied centre. Soult said his men needed less than twenty minutes to reach the Heights, and about fifteen minutes later Napoleon ordered the attack with the words 'One sharp blow and the war is over.' St. Hilaire's division was forced back by delayed Allied detachments and Austrians, then struck again and cleared the Heights. Vandamme's division broke several Allied battalions at the Old Vineyards.</p><p>Grand Duke Constantine's Russian Imperial Guard counterattacked and defeated a battalion of the 4th Line Regiment, the only French standard lost. Napoleon sent in his own Guard cavalry, and Drouet's division then deployed on the flank. The Russians broke, and Kutuzov was severely wounded. Lannes drove Bagration off the field in the north. St. Hilaire and part of Davout's corps then broke through at Sokolnitz, and the Allied army abandoned the field in all directions.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Austerlitz&#8221; cites where it covers Battle, Battle begins, &quot;One sharp blow and the war is over&quot;, Endgame:</p><ul><li>T. Fisher, The Napoleonic Wars: The Rise and Fall of an Empire (Osprey, 2004)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Allied casualties are given as about 36,000 out of 89,000, about 38 per cent of their effective forces, and French losses at around 9,000 out of 66,000, about 13 per cent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> also lost 186 guns and 45 standards. In one retreat, a famous but disputed episode, Russian troops under Dokhturov withdrew over the frozen Satschan ponds, where French artillery fire broke the ice. Estimates of the guns captured range from as few as 38 to more than 100, and of the dead from 200 to 2,000. Local evidence made public later suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s account was exaggerated: when the lakes were drained days later only two or three bodies and some 150 horses were found.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> signed a truce on 4 December, and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a> followed on 26 December. Austria recognised the French gains of the treaties of Campo Formio and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luneville">Lunéville</a>, ceded land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, paid 40 million francs and ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a>. The Russian army was allowed to withdraw home. Napoleon created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, and Francis dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> went to war with France in 1806. Napoleon gave two million francs to his higher officers and 200 francs to each soldier, with pensions for the widows of the fallen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia.jpg" alt="Alexander I of Russia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexander_I_of_Russia_by_G.Dawe_(1826,_Peterhof)-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">George Dawe, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Accounts of the battle disagree on its numbers. One gives the armies at the start as about 72,000 French and 85,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, another as 66,000 and 89,000, and a third puts the French near 53,000 before reinforcements. Allied casualties are given as about 36,000 and French as around 9,000, and for the Satschan ponds the estimates of the dead range from 200 to 2,000 and of the guns captured from as few as 38 to more than 100.</p><p>Much of what is told of the battle comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. His letter to Joséphine called it a victory over the army 'commanded by the two emperors', though Francis was not on the field. His proclamation claimed that a Russian army of seventy-six thousand men had been annihilated, a figure that fits none of these strengths. His account of the ponds is thought exaggerated: when the lakes were drained a few days after the battle, the bodies of only two or three men were found, with some 150 horses, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> Alexander attested to the incident after the wars. Russian data confirm heavy losses at the ponds without giving a number.</p><p>Other matters are later tradition or dispute. The saying 'Never interrupt your enemy when he is making a mistake' derives from a story first recorded by Archibald Alison in 1836 and is called apocryphal. Blame for the Allied defeat was assigned differently: it has been placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> and Weyrother for accepting a battlefield of Napoleon's choosing, while the Tsar himself blamed Kutuzov afterwards.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/victor-hugo.jpg" alt="Victor Hugo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Victor_Hugo_by_%C3%89tienne_Carjat_1876_-_full.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Étienne Carjat, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Austerlitz is often cited by military historians as one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s tactical masterpieces, in the same league as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cannae">Cannae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gaugamela">Gaugamela</a>, and it was the decisive engagement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-third-coalition">War of the Third Coalition</a>. It did not bring a lasting peace: it drove neither <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> nor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to settle, and Prussian worries about French influence led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-fourth-coalition">War of the Fourth Coalition</a> in 1806. Some historians suggest Napoleon was so successful that he lost touch with reality, and that French foreign policy became a personal one after the battle. He did not defeat the Allied army as thoroughly as he wanted. It has also been argued that Kutuzov's plan to retreat further might have let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> be reinforced by Archduke Charles's troops and the Prussians join the Coalition.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the battle was revered in the 19th century by authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-hugo">Victor Hugo</a>. Napoleon never awarded a victory title for it. Government bonds rose from 45 to 66 per cent of face value on the news. At the bicentennial in 2005 neither President Chirac nor Prime Minister de Villepin attended commemorations, and some residents of France's overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> protested against what they saw as the official commemoration of Napoleon.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a> made the battle the close of the first volume of War and Peace. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendome-column">Colonne Vendôme</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was first called the Column of Austerlitz, and the Pont and Gare d'Austerlitz carry its name. Since 1992 the battlefield has been a protected landscape monument zone, with the Cairn of Peace Memorial, built in 1910 to 1912 and dedicated in 1923, near Prace.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Austerlitz on 2 December 1805 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s army broke the centre of a larger Russian and Austrian army after luring it off the Pratzen Heights. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> left the war within weeks by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Pressburg</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> did not settle.</em></p>

